{:title
 "Testing the Smile Prompt Language, from Prompt Metrics in 2023 to Hypotheses, Ablations and Backtests in 2026",
 :description
 "How testing grew in the Smile Prompt Language, from 2023 metrics that cannot all be maximised, through benchmarks and leaderboards in 2025, to research hypotheses, permutation tests, verifiable failures and backtests in 2026, in my own words with every source.",
 :keywords
 "prompt testing, prompt evaluation, LLM as a judge, prompt benchmarks, ablation testing prompts, verifiable prompt failures, prompt backtesting, Smile hypotheses, history of Smile",
 :lede
 "In THE MAP, a test is an operation modelled on the tests I actually ran, a prompt file and a results file paired by name, named for what varies, with the trial condition recorded, run repeatedly so consistency across runs is the finding.",
 :paras
 ["THE MAP measures syntax leak, format fidelity, emphasis applied, name used or invented and role embodiment, and makes every dimension a setting so tens of thousands of variants can be generated and read. It names tests still to run, a vow kept over a duration, a language used as a lens, a banned word list read as a curve over context length, and replay of a past decision against the ledger. Two axes are scored together, whether every face reads as Smile and whether it uses the register the version binds, and the gap between them is where a new version comes from."
 "On 4 October 2023 I asked for metrics of a particular kind, “ensuring semantically distinct metrics that are impossible to harmonize together, so we are not aiming for max on all metrics”, and for evaluations that are “measurable, numbers, True/False's, scales, all kinds of different measurements.”[[m:2023-10-04]] On 31 October the method had its loop, “failure conditions (when is it not working), success conditions (when is it working), will evaluate these and iterate until all failure conditions solved”.[[m:2023-10-31]]"
 "In July 2025 the first questions were about the language itself. On a recording, “Which token is the best start token? I want to test it.”[[m:2025-07-03#2]] The v1 requirements wrote a hypothesis, “even basic smile adoption will massively increase benchmark performance.”, and planned to compare Smile with a neutral baseline and with sad faces.[[m:2025-07-09]] On 10 July, “I'm also planning to scientifically measure the advantage of smiling over not smiling.”[[m:2025-07-10#2]] The Claude Smile document imagined a “Smile leaderboard — track your prompts’ performance vs others on open benchmarks”.[[m:2025-07-14]]"
 "By late 2025 testing became part of the tools. A planning document of October listed “Automatic multi-turn testing: how many turns does the model follow the instructions?” and “An automatic LLM as a judge on your outputs”.[[m:2025-10-29]] In January 2026 I named what I wanted, “I think really what I want to do here is prompt engineering science.”[[m:2026-01-27]]"
 "In February 2026 Smile gave testing its most exact tool, failures a program can see. “if the model puts prompt instruction language in the output, then it hasn’t understood the smile.”, and “no name tag, not using sections, using a sad face not a smile ( ]= instead of [= ) are all verifiable points of failure”.[[m:2026-02-19]] In March, “we can get a lot of verifiably with programmatic, not semantic checks”, so an AI can iterate faster.[[m:2026-03-05]] In April the hypotheses were written as research, among them “Hypothesis 2: Emoticon-based delimiters shift the generation distribution toward positive, cooperative output compared to neutral or negative delimiters”, with the aim that “the ones that survive testing are genuinely established.”[[m:2026-04-05]] In April too I looked for the smallest signal, “the minimal tokens it needs for example, for it to understand it is in a kind interaction”.[[m:2026-04-07]]"
 "On my PC I asked for tests as suites and permutations. In July 2026 I asked that my name tag experiments be ablation tested, and “make it a test suite we can rerun anytime things change.”[[m:2026-07-25]] In August, “I like when we continue to build out the automated permutations aspect”, testing many thousands of variations,[[m:2026-08-05]] and new questions, “does vowing to do something make it happen longer, more likely, more influence than committing?”[[m:2026-08-15]] and “When does the model read a face as sarcastic?”[[m:2026-08-16]] On 19 August I set the conditions of every test, wholesome placeholders and every prompt shown to me before it runs.[[m:2026-08-19]]"
 "In the autumn I asked for testing across my whole archive. On 28 September, “We will backtest this with every single Smile, osrs, DrThomasAger claude code chat historically and in the future.”[[m:2026-09-28]] On 29 September, “Because testing and automatic optimization of prompts etc is so rare”.[[m:2026-09-29]] In October the method was fixed for every instance, “Test small modules constnatly with the REPL to confirm outputs.”, through hypothesis, spec, architecture and test,[[m:2026-10-05]] and “every prompt needs a spec we can verify against”.[[m:2026-10-04]] On 11 October, “we basically need a massive historic archive of every time a prompt was successful and unsuccessful.”[[m:2026-10-11]]"
 "Across {{moments}} moments and {{quotes}} quotes, from {{first}} to {{last}}, testing moved from scoring one prompt to the science of a language and the backtesting of an archive. The tools brought in only after they pass these tests are followed in [[t:bringing-in-the-best]]."],
 :faq
 [["How is a Smile prompt tested?"
 "In THE MAP, by verifiable failures a program can check, such as a missing name tag, missing sections, or prompt language appearing in the reply, together with LLM judges and benchmarks for what only meaning can show. Instruction Checks tests name tag use today, and the rest is part built."]
 ["What hypotheses does Smile test?"
 "Among them that novel delimiters receive more attention, that emoticon delimiters shift replies toward positive and cooperative output, that a model learns a custom family of symbols inside one prompt, and that separating prompt and response language reduces format contamination."]
 ["What is backtesting in Smile?"
 "Running a new method, such as the harvest of a finished chat, across every past conversation of a project, so its results can be checked against what really happened."]]}
